description: On llvm, every constant is built at startup by its own init fn, even a keyword. Each one is analysed (rt-init-fn!), lowered through llvm-fn and run once at start. Plain constants should be static data in the module, as strings and Types (D218) already are.
 
 The world dump on 2026-10-28 (llvm-phase-times with the assemble patch) has 541 constant init fns:
 - 417 keywords: one kw-of-bytes call each;
 - 120 var stand-ins: a symbol plus its metadata map, 2,339 continuation pieces between them;
 - 4 empty collections.
 
 They are 500 of the 833 fns lowered at assemble (4.5 s of 36.9 s before the patch), and const-global!'s analysis of them takes another 2.5 s. Var stand-ins cost about 16 ms each.
 
 Proposal, in steps:
 1. Keywords and symbols without metadata: a static Kw or Sym global over static Bytes, its hash computed at compile time with the runtime's hash-unencoded. One startup loop inserts the static objects into the intern table (rt/llvm/str.lg), so a keyword made at run time with the same text finds the static one. The collector already skips static objects (D215).
 2. Var stand-ins: the symbol static, its metadata map static once maps can be (a small array map needs no hashing), or the map alone left to an init fn.
 3. Vectors, then maps and sets: static only where the compiler can lay them out as the runtime would; the rest keep init fns.
 
 Open questions:
 - Insert at startup, or emit the intern table prefilled (no startup work, but the compiler must reproduce the table's layout and growth)?
 - Does a static keyword's hash need a check against the runtime's at build time (a row comparing hashes for a corpus of names)?
 
 Acceptance: the llvm corpora, P12.31 and the world dump (seeds 1–20) match native lg; the world dump's constant init fns fall from 541 to the var stand-ins and collections left; assemble and startup times recorded with their conditions.
 
 Related: each keyword init tail-calls kw-of-bytes with its own k, yet still emits a continuation piece nothing calls (417 dead functions in the world dump). That is a separate fix in the lowering of a call whose result is returned.
 
 Refs git-issue:7be2318 (llvm compile cost), git-issue:3e08842 (function cache).
